WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.640
Build security determines what enters the container supply chain.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.020
Dockerfiles and similar build definitions should be reviewed for base image choices,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.960
package installation patterns, user configuration, copied files, exposed ports,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.820
entry points, and build-time assumptions.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.840
Build context control matters because accidental inclusion of source trees, credentials,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.580
configuration files,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.320
or temporary artifacts can place sensitive material inside an image or expose it to the

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.580
build system.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.280
Reproducible build concepts help teams understand and trust their artifacts.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.580
Dependency pinning, controlled package sources, deterministic build steps,

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.240
and clear artifact naming make it easier to investigate what changed.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.780
Multi-stage builds separate build-time dependencies from smaller runtime artifacts,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.160
reducing the amount of tooling and intermediate content that ships to production.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.180
The runtime image should contain what is needed to run,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.400
not everything needed to compile or test.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.100
Secrets during build are a common failure path.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.240
Credentials used to fetch private dependencies, publish artifacts,

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.020
or access registries should not be baked into image layers, printed in logs,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.200
or stored in build arguments that become visible later.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.300
CI/CD systems should provide secure secret injection patterns and limit who can read or

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.600
modify pipeline configuration.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.140
The build system is part of the production trust chain.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.880
Pipeline protections include branch protections, required review,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.340
artifact integrity checks, automated scanning, registry publishing controls,

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.160
and policy gates.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.940
The point is not to make every build painful.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.120
The point is to prevent untrusted code, leaked secrets, unreviewed artifacts,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.780
or known risky images from moving quietly into production.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.440
Build security gives runtime security a trustworthy starting point.
